[
    {
        "content": "<p>We now have a page documenting <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/extras/congr.html\">how to use the <code>congr()</code> elaborator</a>. I learned a lot of cool functionality in the course of writing this documentation.</p>\n<p>Did you know <code>congr()</code> supports local variables and can rewrite under binders?</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Tactic</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">TermCongr</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">BigOperators</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Group</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Finset</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Defs</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Finset</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hfg</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hst</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">∑</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∑</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">congr</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">∑</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"n\">hst</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hfg</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"o\">))</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Did you know <code>congr()</code> can prove function extensionality?</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Tactic</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">TermCongr</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">congr</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">))</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I would love to see some of this power reflected in Mathlib. So I'd like to encourage you all to read the new <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/extras/congr.html\"><code>congr()</code> how-to</a> and discover new tricks.</p>",
        "id": 591788456,
        "sender_full_name": "Anne Baanen",
        "timestamp": 1777467591
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Should we also mention the <code>congrm</code> tactic?</p>",
        "id": 591807047,
        "sender_full_name": "Weiyi Wang",
        "timestamp": 1777471508
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Is this like a shortcut to use the kabstract function for a specific subexpression in nice lean syntax?</p>",
        "id": 591811234,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1777472414
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"873350\">Weiyi Wang</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/New.3A.20.60congr.28.29.60.20elaborator.20documentation/near/591807047\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Should we also mention the <code>congrm</code> tactic?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Good suggestion! <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/leanprover-community.github.io/pull/833\">website#833</a> adds <code>congrm</code> to the list of related tactics.</p>",
        "id": 591812212,
        "sender_full_name": "Anne Baanen",
        "timestamp": 1777472632
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"466334\">Shreyas Srinivas</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/New.3A.20.60congr.28.29.60.20elaborator.20documentation/near/591811234\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Is this like a shortcut to use the kabstract function for a specific subexpression in nice lean syntax?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Not really, <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Lean.Meta.kabstract#doc\">docs#Lean.Meta.kabstract</a> with pattern <code>p</code> in the expression <code>e</code> looks for occurrences of <code>p</code> in <code>e</code> and replaces them with a fresh variable. <code>congr()</code> builds an equality proof by composing smaller proofs and <code>rfl</code>s. <code>kabstract</code> is used in (the current, apparently soon to be replaced) <code>rw</code> tactic, and <code>rw</code> also deals with equality, but the similarity ends there.</p>",
        "id": 591813565,
        "sender_full_name": "Anne Baanen",
        "timestamp": 1777472896
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Maybe we should add some more practical examples used in mathlib. Personally I use <code>simpa using congr(...)</code> very often.</p>",
        "id": 591830600,
        "sender_full_name": "Jz Pan",
        "timestamp": 1777476796
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Is this webpage discoverable as part of a more general documentation, or is it just a stand-alone page?</p>",
        "id": 591835970,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1777478157
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks for writing this howto <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"238446\">@Anne Baanen</span>! I too hope to see more use of it — why make brittle proofs with the <code>congr</code>/<code>congr_arg</code>/<code>congr_fun</code> lemmas (which can't handle many kinds of dependent types we see in Mathlib), when you can instead use a <code>congr(...)</code> quotation to express what you want?</p>\n<p>I think it's also neat how Lean 4 really lets us blur the line between terms and tactics. On one hand it represents a proof term, but on the other it's figuring out what that proof term should be based on the specifics of the situation.</p>\n<p>If we use it enough, it might justify getting some more core support to make <code>congr(...)</code> even better <span aria-label=\"slight smile\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f642\" role=\"img\" title=\"slight smile\">:slight_smile:</span></p>",
        "id": 591838777,
        "sender_full_name": "Kyle Miller",
        "timestamp": 1777478792
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>It might be worth adding a section about migrating to <code>congr(...)</code> <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"238446\">@Anne Baanen</span>, at least in a general educational sense. These are from <code>MathlibTest/TermCongr.lean</code>, and maybe it's possible to write them using a <code>#check</code> or <code>show_term</code> to demonstrate how these create the expected <code>congrArg</code>, <code>congrFun</code>, and <code>congr</code> proof terms, respectively.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Sort</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">congr</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Sort</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Sort</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">congr</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Sort</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hx</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">congr</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"n\">hf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"n\">hx</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>It might be worth briefly pointing out that the <code>congr(if $h then 1 else 0)</code> example in the howto is doing something you can't do with these three basic congruence lemmas — there's a <code>Decidable</code> instance that depends on the proposition being rewritten. Of course, this is an example of something <code>simp</code> can handle.</p>",
        "id": 591838831,
        "sender_full_name": "Kyle Miller",
        "timestamp": 1777478807
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"466334\">Shreyas Srinivas</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/New.3A.20.60congr.28.29.60.20elaborator.20documentation/near/591811234\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Is this like a shortcut to use the kabstract function for a specific subexpression in nice lean syntax?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That would be triangle operator <code>▸</code>.</p>\n<p>Anne already explained it well, but I thought I'd add that in the general area of congruence and rewriting, there are a few processes that tactics put together in various ways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Selecting locations that should be rewritten based on a pattern</li>\n<li>Specializing congruence lemmas (rewrite/simp lemmas) for those locations</li>\n<li>Creating the LHS and RHS terms and, possibly, correcting type errors arising from replacing subterms</li>\n<li>Constructing the congruence lemma between the LHS and RHS</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For example, <code>simp</code> interleaves these processes, visiting only those subterms that <code>simp</code> is able to effectively create equality congruence lemmas for. It corrects certain type errors (e.g. type mismatches in proof terms, or in <code>Decidable</code> instances) by following the shapes of the congruence lemmas it generates, rather than being an active process.</p>\n<p>The <code>rw</code> tactic currently is entirely a user interface for using <code>kabstract</code> as a method to generate an <code>Eq.rec</code>-based congruence lemma. Connecting it to <code>congr(...)</code>, it's using <code>kabstract</code> on an expression to find the locations where <code>$</code>'s should go.</p>\n<p>The <code>congr(...)</code> term elaborator on the other hand is not about finding locations or specializing lemmas. It's also not even about rewriting per se. It's about taking an arbitrary term with some pre-specified locations and then interleaving elaboration of the term to get the LHS and RHS with elaboration of the lemmas at these <code>$</code> holes, to get a congruence lemma, which you can do whatever you want with. Since it elaborates the LHS and RHS separately, it handles common cases where you'd need type correction (e.g. those <code>Decidable</code> instances are the elaborated ones, and the only thing <code>congr(...)</code> needs to do is prove they're <code>HEq</code>).</p>\n<p>Connection to <code>rw</code>: Let's say you have <code>rw [h]</code> where the goal is <code>a + b</code> and <code>h : a = a'</code>. At the cost of identifying the positions you wish to rewrite, you can replace this with <code>Eq.mpr</code> plus <code>congr(...)</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a'</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"c1\">-- rw [h]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">apply</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">congr</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mpr</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"c1\">-- ⊢ p (a' + b)</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 591844955,
        "sender_full_name": "Kyle Miller",
        "timestamp": 1777480351
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"366779\">Jz Pan</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/New.3A.20.60congr.28.29.60.20elaborator.20documentation/near/591830600\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Maybe we should add some more practical examples used in mathlib. Personally I use <code>simpa using congr(...)</code> very often.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I'm guessing you use this to do rewrites that <code>simp</code> cannot do? And that <code>rw</code> can't do either, possibly because it's under a binder?</p>",
        "id": 591845214,
        "sender_full_name": "Kyle Miller",
        "timestamp": 1777480435
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"306601\">Kyle Miller</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/New.3A.20.60congr.28.29.60.20elaborator.20documentation/near/591845214\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"366779\">Jz Pan</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/New.3A.20.60congr.28.29.60.20elaborator.20documentation/near/591830600\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Maybe we should add some more practical examples used in mathlib. Personally I use <code>simpa using congr(...)</code> very often.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I'm guessing you use this to do rewrites that <code>simp</code> cannot do? And that <code>rw</code> can't do either, possibly because it's under a binder?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For example, if <code>h</code> is the proof that the composition of some functions is equal to the composition of some other functions. Some of them are <code>Equiv</code>. Suppose I want to prove that the composition of some another functions is equal to the composition of some yet another functions. The proof usually will be <code>ext x; simpa using congr(f ($h (g.symm x)))</code>.</p>",
        "id": 591981278,
        "sender_full_name": "Jz Pan",
        "timestamp": 1777535943
    }
]